{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01QGnnsfCDmvDHZWqH9S99Wf","type":"tool_result","content":"14:## 1. The transformations that generalised\n20:### 1.1 \"Read B once\" — amortise a weight read across every row that uses it\n43:### 1.2 Thread-per-output is wrong at decode shapes\n56:### 1.3 Stage more per barrier\n67:## 2. Structural wins\n69:### 2.1 Full-step CUDA graph (F44)\n78:### 2.2 The arena (F44, F83)\n93:### 2.3 Adaptive verify width (F49, F59, F63)\n102:### 2.4 Fork/join the kv chain (F57)\n131:### 2.5 Don't recompute a prefix that cannot have changed — cache the main-KV (ladder 1.0, 2026-08-20)\n230:### 2.6 A selection sort is not a top-k — single-CTA radix select (ladder 1.2, 2026-08-20)\n341:### 2.7 Change which kernel the bit-exactness claim is *against* — `index_score` as a register-tiled GEMM (ladder 1.5, 2026-08-20)\n413:### 2.8 The one adopted win that is not a kernel — actually serve the head you trained (ladder 2.2, 2026-08-20)\n440:### 2.9 `sparse_attn` — stage the gathered row in shared memory, and vectorise the load without losing bit-exactness (ladder 1.7, 2026-08-20)\n515:## 3. Precision and layout\n548:## 4. What every adopted change has in common\n=== NEG ===\n12:## 1. The most expensive lesson: a fake +28 % that passed every gate\n33:## 2. Retired by measurement\n52:## 3. The negatives that were really instrumentation failures\n71:## 4. Conditionally retired — re-open after the draft-head fine-tune\n88:## 4b. Killed inside the winning iteration — the radix top-k's two dead levers (ladder 1.2, 2026-08-20)\n119:## 4c. Adopted, bit-exact, and worth nothing — the `lim <= topk` early-out (ladder 1.3, 2026-08-20)\n154:## 4d. Superseded before it shipped — the tiled `index_score`, and the cost of picking the wrong reference (ladder 1.5, 2026-08-20)\n195:## 4e. The lever that was already applied — `jetson_clocks` (ladder 3.1, 2026-08-20)\n242:## 4f. Two hypotheses killed on the way to localising the prefill race (ladder 1.9, 2026-08-20)\n270:## 4g. Retired on a null before it was built on — `hpb` alone, and the reuse hypothesis (ladder 1.7, 2026-08-20)\n301:## 4h. Headroom that was never there — the `cattn:q_proj` \"free 4.4 ms\" (ladder 1.8, 2026-08-20)\n330:## 4i. Packing the KV cache is a CAPACITY lever, not a throughput one — and the kernel it feeds says why (ladder 1b.2, 2026-08-20)\n388:## 5. What the negatives taught\n=== TRAPS ===\n95:## 9. Self-checks that cry wolf are still worth keeping\n102:## 10. Process traps in the harness itself\n116:## 11. Gate the shape range the engine spans\n125:## 12. The engine stops reproducing itself part-way through a long run, so \"bit-identical token ids\" is not a test there\n179:## 13. A gate that passed, reported as a failure, because the probe it rode on kept no records\n200:## 14. A phase that ran nothing, and reported \"finished\"\n239:## 15. Put a leg the change *cannot* touch inside the same sweep\n278:## 16. A fitted slope is only \"cost per 1000 context\" if the mark is linear in context\n313:## 17. A launch that failed, and a `cudaStreamSynchronize` that called it success\n393:## 18. The device opt-in maximum is not the kernel's maximum, and the safety margin that failed a passing gate\n428:## 19. The variance is BETWEEN checkpoint loads, not within them — 0.6 % vs 5.7 %\n471:## 20. A gate that does not compile is a gate that passes by never running (ladder 1.5, 2026-08-20)\n519:## 21. dprof reads a saving 15–20 % low, so it attributes but does not ratchet (ladder 1.5)\n542:## 22. `tau` reproduced to four decimals; `tok/s` on the same protocol did not — and the promotion gate keys on the second one (ladder 2.2, 2026-08-20)\n564:### 22.1 The consequence for the selection rule\n588:### 22.2 The 17 % that is not in the answer but should be in someone's notebook\n606:## 23. A drift control that failed — and the agent that was contaminating its own arms (ladder 3.1, 2026-08-20)\n655:## 24. The base-AR window times the governor, not the engine — and it is 21 % (ladder 3.1, 2026-08-20)\n692:## 25. A unit gate that stops six times short of the regime it is quoted about (ladder 1.9, 2026-08-20)\n721:## 26. A field that was never computed compares equal, and equal reads as exonerated (ladder 1.9)\n749:## 27. `build/decode` and `dsv4-server` do not share a prefill, and a bit-exactness result on one says nothing about the other (ladder 1.9)\n770:## 28. A tuning default that was a regression at its own design point, and the mechanism story that was wrong (ladder 1.7, 2026-08-20)\n814:## 29. A dprof mark that timed another stream's work, and reported it as a 3.2x swing in a GEMM (ladder 1.8, 2026-08-20)\n880:## 30. A two-arm gate sweep in which the arms were identical by construction (ladder 1b.2, 2026-08-20)\n909:## 31. The resident-set line cannot price a cache that has not been touched (ladder 1b.2, 2026-08-20)","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"da811920-c880-42ff-a202-5749e1a7ccdc","uuid":"b6349e2a-55c2-4d0e-a875-5b502144b290","timestamp":"2026-08-20T22:11:51.047Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"14:## 1. The transformations that generalised\n20:### 1.1 \"Read B once\" — amortise a weight read across every row that uses it\n43:### 1.2 Thread-per-output is wrong at decode shapes\n56:### 1.3 Stage more per barrier\n67:## 2. Structural wins\n69:### 2.1 Full-step CUDA graph (F44)\n78:### 2.2 The arena (F44, F83)\n93:### 2.3 Adaptive verify width (F49, F59, F63)\n102:### 2.4 Fork/join the kv chain (F57)\n131:### 2.5 Don't recompute a prefix that cannot have changed — cache the main-KV (ladder 1.0, 2026-08-20)\n230:### 2.6 A selection sort is not a top-k — single-CTA radix select (ladder 1.2, 2026-08-20)\n341:### 2.7 Change which kernel the bit-exactness claim is *against* — `index_score` as a register-tiled GEMM (ladder 1.5, 2026-08-20)\n413:### 2.8 The one adopted win that is not a kernel — actually serve the head you trained (ladder 2.2, 2026-08-20)\n440:### 2.9 `spars